{"id":"n-20261029-a4igjt","category":"sports","display_title":"English Rugby Focuses on Domestic Expansion","display_title_he":"הרוגבי האנגלי מתמקד בהתרחבות מקומית","short_summary":"English rugby governing bodies have stated their priority is to add domestic clubs rather than Welsh teams, despite reports of interest from the Welsh Rugby Union.","english_level":"C1","words":461,"reading_time":3,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Rugby_Tavern%2C_Bloomsbury%2C_WC1_%282843227171%29.jpg/960px-Rugby_Tavern%2C_Bloomsbury%2C_WC1_%282843227171%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Ewan Munro from London, UK · CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rugby_Tavern,_Bloomsbury,_WC1_(2843227171).jpg"},"sources":[{"name":"BBC Sport","domain":"bbc.co.uk","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/rugby-union/articles/c54g1x0z4rygo","title":"Prem eyes English expansion despite Welsh interest","published_at":"2026-10-28T18:55:30.000Z"},{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/28/prem-rfu-commit-english-clubs-after-report-welsh-sides-joining","title":"Prem and RFU commit to expanding with English clubs after report of Welsh sides joining","published_at":"2026-10-28T17:17:04.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-28T17:17:04.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-29T07:13:18.936Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["English rugby authorities have firmly stated that their primary focus remains on domestic growth rather than incorporating teams from Wales. According to joint announcements from Prem Rugby and the Rugby Football Union, current plans involve adding two English clubs to the top-flight competition by the 2029-30 season. This clarification follows media reports suggesting that the Welsh Rugby Union had engaged in advanced discussions regarding a potential cross-border Anglo-Welsh league.","While senior figures within English club rugby acknowledged that the Welsh Rugby Union had indeed expressed an interest in exploring a future within the English club system, they insisted that no formal negotiations were currently taking place. The English top tier was reduced to ten participating sides after Wasps, Worcester, and London Irish were forced to fold during the difficult 2022-23 season. Furthermore, landmark structural changes voted through for English rugby mean that traditional promotion and relegation will be completely scrapped starting from the 2026-27 season onward.","Instead of earning a spot through the second-tier Championship by winning a play-off, prospective clubs will now apply directly and undergo a rigorous evaluation process established by the Men's Professional Rugby Board. Bids will be assessed based on standard of play, financial health, investment potential, infrastructure, and how a team's geographical location might enhance the league's overall commercial appeal. Although the overarching long-term ambition is to grow the competition beyond, initial targets aim for a twelve-team league by the 2029-30 season. English rugby leadership emphasized that their resources are fully dedicated to supporting domestic clubs across the entire pyramid that aspire to ascend to the top flight.","Meanwhile, the Welsh Rugby Union has been navigating severe financial pressures, recently reporting an 18 million pound loss for the financial year, following a £7.2m loss previously. These mounting financial difficulties have reinforced the governing body's argument that maintaining four professional men's regions in Wales is no longer sustainable, especially with a predicted similar loss next year. Gavin Marshall noted that they could only spend £20m on professional rugby compared to the £26m contributed now, and the Prem P-share cost to enter is valued similarly at £20m or up to £23.5m including a women's programme requirement.","Consequently, the WRU intends to press ahead with plans to reduce the number of professional sides to three by 2028, with licenses in the capital, east, and west, and a final decision expected in the summer of 2027 following a December summit. While the Welsh union acknowledged the broad appeal of the English top division—and even pitched for two women's teams, Brython Thunder and Gwalia Lightning, to join Premiership Women's Rugby—its men's teams like Ospreys, Cardiff, Scarlets, and Dragons remain committed to the 16-team United Rugby Championship alongside teams from Scotland, Ireland, Italy, and South Africa until the conclusion of the 2027-28 season."],"facts":[{"fact":"English rugby bosses have confirmed they are focusing on expanding the Prem with English clubs rather than bringing in teams from Wales.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Prem Rugby and the Rugby Football Union released a joint statement stating they were looking at adding two English clubs by 2029.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The Welsh Rugby Union expressed an interest in joining an Anglo-Welsh league, and the Professional Rugby Board was told the WRU plans to explore a future in the English club game.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Senior sources in English club rugby confirmed interest from the WRU, but insisted negotiations are not taking place.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The Prem has been reduced to 10 sides after Wasps, Worcester and London Irish folded during the 2022-23 season.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Traditional promotion and relegation to and from the top tier has been scrapped from the start of the 2026-27 season.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Clubs will instead apply to join and be judged across criteria such as standard of play, finances, investment potential, infrastructure, and geographical base.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The league hopes to initially expand to 12 teams for the 2029-30 season.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The WRU posted an £18m loss for the latest financial year, compared to a £7.2m loss the previous year, with figures backing its case that four professional regions are no longer sustainable.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The WRU plans to cut a professional side by 2028, moving from four teams to three.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"English","lemma":"English","pos":"adj","he":"אנגלית","name":"English"},{"text":"rugby","lemma":"rugby","pos":"n","he":"רוגבי","name":false},{"text":"authorities","lemma":"authority","pos":"n","he":"רשויות","name":false},{"text":"firmly","lemma":"firmly","pos":"adv","he":"באופן נחרץ","name":false},{"text":"stated","lemma":"state","pos":"v","he":"הצהירו","name":false},{"text":"primary","lemma":"primary","pos":"adj","he":"עיקרי","name":false},{"text":"focus","lemma":"focus","pos":"n","he":"מוקד","name":false},{"text":"remains","lemma":"remain","pos":"v","he":"נשאר","name":false},{"text":"domestic","lemma":"domestic","pos":"adj","he":"מקומי","name":false},{"text":"growth","lemma":"growth","pos":"n","he":"צמיחה","name":false},{"text":"rather than","lemma":"rather than","pos":"prep","he":"במקום","name":false},{"text":"incorporating","lemma":"incorporate","pos":"v","he":"לשלב","name":false},{"text":"teams","lemma":"team","pos":"n","he":"קבוצות","name":false},{"text":"Wales","lemma":"Wales","pos":"n","he":"וויילס","name":"Wales"},{"text":"joint","lemma":"joint","pos":"adj","he":"משותף","name":false},{"text":"announcements","lemma":"announcement","pos":"n","he":"הכרזות","name":false},{"text":"Prem Rugby","lemma":"Prem Rugby","pos":"n","he":"פרם רוגבי","name":"Prem Rugby"},{"text":"Rugby Football Union","lemma":"Rugby Football Union","pos":"n","he":"איגוד הרוגבי של אנגליה","name":"Rugby Football Union"},{"text":"current","lemma":"current","pos":"adj","he":"נוכחי","name":false},{"text":"plans","lemma":"plan","pos":"n","he":"תוכניות","name":false},{"text":"involve","lemma":"involve","pos":"v","he":"כוללות","name":false},{"text":"adding","lemma":"add","pos":"v","he":"הוספת","name":false},{"text":"two","lem